.TH ASCII 1 "23rd of November 2026" "zenithutils" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
ascii - print the ascii table, or codes of specified characters

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBascii\fI [-l] [reprs] [codes] ...\fp

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBascii\fP, without any arguments, prints the 128 characters of the ascii table on 8 (11 to 16 char wide) columns and 17 lines.

This is not unlike busybox's version of ascii

If arguments are provided via the command line, show ascii decimal and hexadecimal codes, along with the ascii representation for each argument.

If zenithutils was compiled with ASCII_SHOW_OCTAL, octal codes will also be displayed

.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
  $ ascii -l 1 0x10 0o033 NUL 'a' 0x1
  Dec Hx Oct
   49 31 061 1
   16 10 020 DLE
   27 1B 033 ESC
    0 00 000 NUL
   97 61 141 a
    1 01 001 SOH
.EE

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB-l\fP
take any arguments as literal characters
e.g. 'ascii -l 1' would print ascii for the literal character '1', not code 0x01     
exceptions are hex and octal literals, they are always interpreted as codes

.PP
When configuring the software before building with './configure', pass the option '--enable-ascii-show-octal' enable the display of octal codes 
.PP
Alternatively, in 'config.h', you can do the same by setting \fBASCII_SHOW_OCTAL\fP to 1

.SH SEE ALSO
ascii(7)
